---
title: Canonical Entity Surface
description: A composable capability contract (CRUD, query/filter, soft-delete, versioning, relations, real-time, bulk) with SurrealQL mappings and a roadmap.
---

A contract for how every domain entity (contact, media, calendar, event,
organization, connection, notification, …) is queried and mutated. Today each
entity hand-rolls its own surface and they have drifted apart; this document
defines one composable contract so existing entities can be made consistent and
new ones (e.g. the planned email / notifications / assets apps) get a complete,
predictable surface for free.

This is a **design spec / roadmap**, not yet implemented. The only piece that
exists today is the contact filter compiler
(`storage/database/contact_filter.go`) and the contact query surface; everything
else is the target.

---

## 1. Model: base + opt-in capabilities

An entity has a **base** surface always, then **opts into capabilities**. Each
capability contributes a slice of queries/mutations/inputs. Capabilities compose
— a contact is `Queryable + SoftDeletable + Versioned + Related`; a notification
might be only `Queryable + Subscribable`.

```
Entity = Identity + CRUD
       + [Queryable] + [SoftDeletable] + [Versioned] + [Related] + [Subscribable] + [Bulk]
       × cross-cutting axes (ownership scope, validation, media refs)
```

Capabilities are **declared once** in an `EntityDescriptor` (Go) and drive the
generated surface; see §5.

---

## 2. Base surface (every entity)

**Identity** — `id: ID!`, `created_at`, `updated_at`. Modelled as GraphQL
`interface Node` / `interface Timestamped`.

**CRUD**

| Op | Shape |
|----|-------|
| get | `entity(id: ID!): Entity` |
| create | `createEntity(input: CreateEntityInput!): Entity!` |
| update | `updateEntity(id: ID!, input: UpdateEntityInput!): Entity!` (Versioned entities also take `parentVersionIds` — see §4.3) |
| delete | `deleteEntity(id: ID!): Boolean!` — **hard** delete; for soft delete use the SoftDeletable lifecycle |

---

## 3. Capability: Queryable

Collection reads with filter, search, sort, pagination, and count.

```graphql
entities(
  scope: Scope = ACTIVE      # only if SoftDeletable
  search: String             # sugar over the FTS / contains filter
  filter: EntityFilter
  sort: [SortInput!]
  page: PageInput            # { limit, offset }
): [Entity!]!

entitiesCount(scope: Scope, search: String, filter: EntityFilter): Int!
```

### 3.1 Filter inputs (shared operator types)

Defined once in `_shared.graphql`, reused by every entity's `EntityFilter`:

```graphql
input StringFilter {
  eq ne contains startsWith endsWith in[] isNull
  matches: String     # regex             (string::matches)
  fuzzy:   String     # typo-tolerant     (~ operator / string::similarity::fuzzy)
  search:  String     # full-text, ranked (@@ on an analyzer index)
}
input BoolFilter   { eq }
input IntFilter    { eq gt gte lt lte }
input DateFilter   { eq gt gte lt lte before after withinLast: Duration }
input StringListFilter { has hasAll hasAny isEmpty size: IntFilter }
input GeoFilter        { near: NearInput  within: GeoJSON  isNull: Boolean }   # coordinate fields
input NearInput        { lat: Float!  lng: Float!  radiusMeters: Float! }

# Repeatable object fields (emails/phones) match when ANY element matches. The
# element filter is per-entity (its own field shape); for contacts:
input ContactFieldFilter { value: StringFilter  type: String }               # one email/phone element
input FieldListFilter    { any: ContactFieldFilter  isEmpty: Boolean  size: IntFilter }
```

A per-entity `EntityFilter` maps its own fields onto these and adds boolean
combinators:

```graphql
input ContactFilter {
  formatted_name: StringFilter
  birthday: StringFilter
  emails: FieldListFilter
  phones: FieldListFilter
  favorite: BoolFilter
  geo: GeoFilter
  and: [ContactFilter!]
  or:  [ContactFilter!]
  not: ContactFilter
}
```

> The per-field inputs **must** reference concrete fields, so `EntityFilter` is
> generated per entity (GraphQL has no input generics). The operator inputs and
> the `and/or/not` skeleton are shared.

### 3.2 SurrealQL compilation

Each operator compiles to a parameterized WHERE fragment; operators on one filter
are ANDed; `and/or/not` build the boolean tree with real parentheses. This is the
existing `filterCompiler` (`storage/database/contact_filter.go`) — to be
generalized to take an `EntityDescriptor` (field → column + kind) instead of
hardcoded contact columns.

| Operator | SurrealQL |
|----------|-----------|
| `String.eq` | `col = $p` |
| `String.contains` | `string::lowercase(col) CONTAINS $p` (needle lowercased) |
| `String.startsWith` | `string::starts_with(string::lowercase(col), $p)` |
| `String.matches` | `string::matches(col, $re)` (regex per row; anchor patterns to avoid backtracking) |
| `String.fuzzy` | `col ~ $p` (fuzzy operator); scored variant `string::similarity::fuzzy(string::lowercase(col), $p) > 0` |
| `String.search` | `col @1@ $p` — full-text via an analyzer index, ranked with `search::score(1)` (see below) |
| `String.isNull: true` | `col = NONE` |
| `Int.gt` | `col > $p` |
| `Date.before` | `col < <datetime>$p` |
| `Date.withinLast: 30d` | `col > time::now() - 30d` |
| `StringList.hasAll` | `col CONTAINSALL $p` |
| `StringList.hasAny` | `col CONTAINSANY $p` |
| `FieldList.isEmpty: false` | `array::len(col ?? []) > 0` |
| `FieldList.any.value.contains` | `array::len((col ?? [])[WHERE string::lowercase(value) CONTAINS $p]) > 0` |
| `and` / `or` / `not` | `( … AND … )` / `( … OR … )` / `!( … )` |

Guardrails: cap boolean nesting depth; always apply a `LIMIT`; param names
(`f0, f1, …`) never collide with the query's own params.

These never scan the whole table: every query is **ownership-scoped first** (§9),
so `contains` / `fuzzy` / `matches` only evaluate over the caller's own slice — a
personal contact list, not the global table — which is cheap even though they're
linear in that slice. `search` (below) still wins once a single user's collection
gets large or you want stemming/ranking. `matches` runs a regex per row, so anchor
patterns to avoid pathological backtracking; `fuzzy` is the typo-tolerant `~`
operator (or a `string::similarity::*` /
`string::distance::normalized_levenshtein` threshold when you need a score).

#### Full-text search (`String.search`)

Because reads are already user-scoped, `contains` covers ordinary search at
personal scale. Reach for `search` when a single user's collection is large, or
when you want tokenized + stemmed matching and relevance ranking. It uses an
**analyzer index**, defined once per searchable field (the `EntityDescriptor`
flags which columns have one):

```surql
DEFINE ANALYZER std TOKENIZERS blank,class FILTERS lowercase,ascii,snowball(english);
DEFINE INDEX contact_fts ON contact FIELDS formatted_name SEARCH ANALYZER std BM25 HIGHLIGHTS;
```

`String.search` then compiles to the `@@` match operator with BM25 relevance
ranking (the `@1@` reference ties the match to its score/highlight helpers):

```surql
SELECT *, search::score(1) AS score
FROM   contact
WHERE  formatted_name @1@ $q
ORDER  BY score DESC;
```

`search::highlight(prefix, suffix, 1)` and `search::offsets(1)` return marked-up
snippets for result display. Tokenized + stemmed matching ("organize" matches
"organizing") and ranking are the difference from `contains`. Without an index on
the field, `search` degrades to a `contains` scan.

### 3.3 Sorting & pagination

`SortInput { field, direction: ASC|DESC }` → `ORDER BY`. `PageInput { limit,
offset }` → `LIMIT $limit START $offset`. (Cursor pagination is a future option;
offset is enough at current scale.)

### 3.4 Location / geo filters

For entities with a coordinate field (Contact `geo`, address coordinates), a
`GeoFilter` does radius and region queries. Store the column as a SurrealDB
`geometry<point>` — coordinates are **`(longitude, latitude)`** order — so it can
be spatially indexed; a `{ lat, lng }` object is converted with
`type::point([lng, lat])`.

| Op | SurrealQL |
|----|-----------|
| `near` (within radius) | `geo::distance(col, type::point([$lng, $lat])) <= $radiusMeters` |
| `within` (polygon/region) | `col INSIDE $geojson` |
| nearest-first sort | `ORDER BY geo::distance(col, $point)` |

`geo::distance` returns the haversine distance in **metres**. Point-in-region uses
the geometry operators (`INSIDE` / `OUTSIDE` / `INTERSECTS`), which are SurrealQL
operators, not `geo::` functions. Related helpers if needed:
`geo::bearing(a, b)`, `geo::centroid(geom)`, `geo::area(geom)` (m²),
`geo::hash::encode/decode`, `geo::is_valid(geom)`.

---

## 4. Capability: SoftDeletable

`deleted` / `archived` flags plus a scope enum, and a lifecycle that records a
version (when also Versioned) instead of mutating in place.

```graphql
enum Scope { ACTIVE ARCHIVED TRASHED ALL }

trashEntity(id: ID!): Entity!       # deleted = true
restoreEntity(id: ID!): Entity!     # deleted = false
archiveEntity(id: ID!): Entity!     # archived = true
unarchiveEntity(id: ID!): Entity!   # archived = false
```

Scope → WHERE: `ACTIVE` = `deleted=false AND archived=false`, `ARCHIVED` =
`archived=true`, `TRASHED` = `deleted=true`, `ALL` = no flag filter.

> Consistency target: Organization/ClientDatabase have a delete flag but no
> restore; this capability gives them the full lifecycle uniformly.

---

## 5. Capability: Versioned

The `<entity>` + `<entity>_version` + shared `derived_from` pattern (see the
*Versioning Pattern* section of `apps/api/CLAUDE.md`). The surface is larger than
"history + revert":

### 5.1 Reads

```graphql
entityHistory(id: ID!, filter: VersionFilter): [EntityVersion!]!
entityVersion(versionId: ID!): EntityVersion
entityDiff(versionA: ID!, versionB: ID!): [FieldChange!]!
```

`EntityVersion` mirrors the entity's fields (READONLY) plus `parent_ids: [ID!]`
(lineage). `VersionFilter` reuses the §3.1 field operators **over the snapshot
fields** and adds:

```graphql
input VersionFilter {
  # …the entity's field operators…           # filter over DATA
  before: String   after: String             # filter over TIME
  isHead: Boolean                             # the current head version
  parentOf: ID     childOf: ID                # one hop on derived_from
  ancestorOf: ID   descendantOf: ID           # transitive lineage walk
}
```

This unifies "query entities" and "query their history" under one compiler —
versions just register the extra lineage/time columns.

### 5.2 Lineage navigation

`derived_from` connects `parent_version -> derived_from -> child_version`. A
single edit has one parent; a merge has two. Walks:

| Need | SurrealQL |
|------|-----------|
| direct parents of V | `V<-derived_from<-entity_version` |
| direct children of V | `V->derived_from->entity_version` |
| is V the head | `V = (SELECT version FROM entity WHERE id = $id)` |
| ancestors / descendants | recursive traversal over `derived_from` |

(The branching tree the contacts history UI renders already consumes
`parent_ids`.)

### 5.3 Writes

```graphql
updateEntity(id: ID!, parentVersionIds: [ID!]!, input: UpdateEntityInput!): Entity!
revertEntityVersion(id: ID!, versionId: ID!): Entity!   # FORK: copy old version → new head, parent = that version
mergeEntityVersions(id: ID!, parentVersionIds: [ID!]!, input: UpdateEntityInput!): Entity!  # multi-parent
```

`revert` and `merge` are the two lineage-forking operations: revert forks from one
old version (already implemented for contacts as `restoreContactVersion`), merge
records 2+ parents on one new version.

---

## 6. Capability: Related

Typed graph edges between entities, their metadata, and traversal. Relations are
**plural and typed** — `related` (contact↔contact), `member` (group membership),
`connection` (user↔user) are distinct edge kinds, not one thing.

```graphql
relateEntities(fromId: ID!, toId: ID!, kind: RelationKind!, meta: EdgeMetaInput): Boolean!
unrelateEntities(fromId: ID!, toId: ID!, kind: RelationKind!): Boolean!
updateEdge(fromId: ID!, toId: ID!, kind: RelationKind!, meta: EdgeMetaInput!): Boolean!   # NEW: edit types/pref/role in place
```

> Gap this closes: today you can relate/unrelate but **not edit an existing edge's
> metadata** (`related.types/pref`, `membership.role`). `updateEdge` adds it.

Traversal / filter-by-relation (extends `EntityFilter`):

```graphql
input RelationFilter {
  relatedTo: ID            # has edge to this entity
  memberOf: ID             # is in this group
  hasRelations: IntFilter  # e.g. count >= 3
  kind: RelationKind
}
```

| Need | SurrealQL |
|------|-----------|
| related-to X | `$x IN ->related->entity.id` |
| member-of group G | `$g IN <-member<-` / `G->member->entity CONTAINS $id` |
| has ≥N relations | `count(->related->entity) >= $n` |

---

## 7. Capability: Subscribable (real-time)

Currently **absent everywhere** — clients poll. SurrealDB provides `LIVE SELECT`;
expose it as GraphQL subscriptions.

```graphql
type Subscription {
  entityChanged(id: ID!): EntityEvent!
  entitiesChanged(scope: Scope, filter: EntityFilter): EntityEvent!
}
type EntityEvent { action: CREATE|UPDATE|DELETE, entity: Entity }
```

Backed by a `LIVE SELECT … WHERE <scope+filter>` per subscriber, multiplexed
through a subscription manager; the SDK gains a subscription transport. This is
the largest new axis (transport + lifecycle + auth on the live channel) and is
sequenced last (P6).

---

## 8. Capability: Bulk

Server-side batch instead of client-side loops (how import / trash-many work
today).

```graphql
createEntities(inputs: [CreateEntityInput!]!): BulkResult!
updateEntities(ids: [ID!]!, input: UpdateEntityInput!): BulkResult!
trashEntities(ids: [ID!]!): BulkResult!
deleteEntities(ids: [ID!]!): BulkResult!
type BulkResult { succeeded: Int!, failed: [BulkError!]! }
```

Run inside one SurrealDB transaction where atomicity matters; partial-success
reporting otherwise.

---

## 9. Cross-cutting axes (apply to every method)

- **Ownership scope** — every query/mutation is scoped to the caller, and this is
  the **primary access path**: the scope predicate runs first, so a read only ever
  touches the caller's own slice (one user's contacts), never the whole table.
  Keep the ownership column indexed (`DEFINE INDEX … ON <entity> FIELDS user`) so
  that narrowing is a lookup, not a scan — then the secondary filters evaluate over
  a small set and stay cheap. Three shapes: **user-scoped**
  (`user = $claim.subject` — contact, notification, connection), **org-scoped**
  (membership check — calendar, event, client), and **hybrid-via-parent** (scoped
  through an owning record — client_database via client→org). The descriptor
  declares which.
- **Validation** — per-entity input validation runs before any write.
- **Media refs** — fields that hold encrypted-media ids (e.g. `photo`, `logo`)
  resolve through the Vault manifest flow; references are opaque ids in the
  entity, resolved out-of-band by the client.

---

## 10. Where "define once" lands (per layer)

| Layer | Shared (write once) | Per-entity (generated/concrete) |
|-------|---------------------|---------------------------------|
| **Go** | `filterCompiler` (entity-generic via `EntityDescriptor`), `VersionedStore[Row]` with list/lifecycle/history/restore/merge/count/relation/subscribe, lineage + relation helpers | `EntityDescriptor` (table, fields→columns+kinds, capabilities, scope), the row struct |
| **GraphQL** | operator inputs, `Scope`, `interface Node/Versioned`, `Page`/`Sort`, `VersionFilter` skeleton, `BulkResult` | `EntityFilter`, `EntityVersion`, lifecycle mutations (boilerplate) |
| **SDK** | `VersionedResource<Sel>` base (list/get/history/restore/relate/subscribe) | selection set + resource name |

GraphQL can't generic the per-entity inputs, so those are **codegen'd from the
descriptor** once there are ~3 entities; hand-written before that. The biggest
genuine write-once win is the Go layer (compiler + store) and the shared operator
inputs.

---

## 11. Current state vs. target

| Entity | Query+filter | Soft-delete | Versioned | Related | Real-time | Bulk |
|--------|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| contact | ✅ (filter built) | ✅ full | ✅ (+revert) | ✅ relate/member | ❌ | ❌ |
| media | ❌ | partial | ✅ | refs only | ❌ | ❌ |
| calendar | ❌ no pagination | ❌ | ❌ | ref | ❌ | ❌ |
| event | range only | ❌ | ❌ | refs | ❌ | ❌ |
| organization | ❌ | flag, no restore | ❌ | membership | ❌ | ❌ |
| connection | status filter | ❌ | ❌ | edges (status) | ❌ | ❌ |
| notification | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |

Legend: ✅ present · partial · ❌ absent. The contract makes each column uniform
for entities that opt into the capability.

---

## 12. Implementation roadmap

Sequenced low-risk → high-effort. Each phase is independently shippable.

- **P1** — generalize `filterCompiler` + introduce `EntityDescriptor`; generic
  `VersionedStore[Row]`; refactor `ContactStore` onto it; prove on `media`; add
  `count`. *(Server-only, no schema churn.)*
- **P2** — shared GraphQL primitives (`_shared.graphql`); make Queryable +
  SoftDeletable consistent across existing entities (pagination for
  Calendar/Event/Notification; restore for Organization).
- **P3** — complete Versioned surface generically: `version(id)`, `diff`, lineage
  navigation, `merge`.
- **P4** — Related surface: `updateEdge`, relation traversal + filter-by-relation.
- **P5** — Bulk mutations.
- **P6** — Real-time: `LIVE SELECT` + GraphQL subscriptions + SDK transport.
- **P7** — codegen per-entity SDL/resolvers from descriptors; SDK
  `VersionedResource` base.

### SurrealDB operators available for future filter growth

First-class in the contract: text (`contains`/`startsWith`/`endsWith`/`matches`/
`fuzzy`/full-text `search`), `CONTAINSALL/ANY` lists, date ranges, and geo
(`near`/`within`). Still on the menu when a need shows up: `CONTAINSNONE`,
`INSIDE/ALLINSIDE/ANYINSIDE` for non-geo arrays, edit-distance thresholds
(`string::distance::normalized_levenshtein`), date-part functions (`time::year`,
`time::wday`), `string::is::*` format validators, and KNN vector search (`<|K|>`,
needs embeddings + an MTREE/HNSW index). Expose deliberately: ownership scoping
keeps most filters cheap over the caller's slice, but each operator is still a
maintenance surface (and `search`/geo want an index), so grow on demand rather
than exposing everything SurrealQL can express.
